- op: discuss  # 93ea
  at: '2026-03-19T00:46:31Z'  # 93ea
  by: agent  # 93ea
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 93ea
  clock: 6  # 93ea
  nonce: 93ea  # 93ea
  message: "Not pointless, but lower priority than originally expected. The I/O boundary map already traverses FFI edges because the existing linkers (pyffi, cgo, jni, napi, wasm_bindgen) create cross-language call edges, and the boundary tagging pass stamps io_boundary on ANY call edge whose target matches an I/O primitive — regardless of which linker created the edge.\n\nWhat dedicated FFI integration would add: when a Python call crosses into C via pyffi, and the C code calls fopen(), the boundary map would show the chain: python_func → c_func → fopen [fs_read]. Currently, the C-side fopen() is only tagged if the C analyzer produced a call edge to fopen — which it does in most cases.\n\nSo the existing behavior works for the common case. Explicit FFI integration would help for edge cases where the C/FFI target function isn't directly tagged (e.g., a C extension that wraps fopen inside a custom function). That's real but low-frequency."  # 93ea
